<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#german-emperor">Glossary</a> · Term · title</p><h1 class="enc-title">German Emperor</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1871">1871</a>–<a href="/years/1918">1918</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Deutscher Kaiser</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">German Emperor was the title of the head of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-empire">German Empire</a> from 1871 to 1918, always held by the King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a>. William I was proclaimed German Emperor in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hall-of-mirrors">Hall of Mirrors</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/versailles">Versailles</a> on 18 January 1871, followed by Frederick III and William II, whose abdication in November 1918 ended the title.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/german-emperor.jpg" alt="German Emperor" width="320" height="399" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Original: Emil Doepler Vector: David Liuzzo · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Wappen_Deutsches_Reich_-_Reichsadler_1889.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/revolutions-of-1848">revolutions of 1848</a> the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankfurt-parliament">Frankfurt Parliament</a> offered King Friedrich Wilhelm IV of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> the title Emperor of the Germans in 1849. He declined, holding that it was not the Parliament's to give and that only the German princes could make such an offer, as in the tradition of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a>.</p><p>The later title was chosen with care by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/otto-von-bismarck">Otto von Bismarck</a>, Minister President of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> and Chancellor of the North German Confederation. Wilhelm I accepted it grudgingly on 18 January 1871, at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/versailles">Versailles</a> during the Siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>. He had preferred Emperor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, but that would have claimed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">territorial sovereignty</a> unacceptable to the South German monarchs and a claim to lands such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a>. He rejected Emperor of the Germans because he believed he ruled by divine right, not by the will of the people, and he disliked a crown created by a constitution, fearing it would overshadow the Prussian one.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The constitution of 1 January 1871 turned the North German Confederation into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-empire">German Empire</a>, a federal monarchy. The emperor was head of state and president of the federated monarchs, among them the kings of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a>, Württemberg and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/free-imperial-city">free cities</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamburg">Hamburg</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lubeck">Lübeck</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bremen">Bremen</a>. The presidency was permanently held by the king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a>, so the imperial crown was tied directly to the Prussian crown. Since 1867 the presidency of the Confederation had already been hereditary in the Prussian kings. The emperors also bore a long list of titles reflecting the lands of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-hohenzollern">House of Hohenzollern</a>, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/margrave-of-brandenburg">Margrave of Brandenburg</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgraviate-of-nuremberg">Burgrave of Nuremberg</a> and Count of Hohenzollern.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wilhelm-ii">Wilhelm II</a> discovered the link between the two crowns in 1918. He wrongly believed he ruled the empire in personal union with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> and thought he could keep the Prussian crown. His last chancellor, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-maximilian-of-baden">Prince Max of Baden</a>, knew this was legally impossible and on 9 November announced the abdication of both thrones. That night Wilhelm went into exile in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a>, and on 28 November he formally gave up all claims. After the revolution the head of state was the president of the Reich, beginning with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/friedrich-ebert">Friedrich Ebert</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “German Emperor” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Wilhelm I of Prussia was declared <b>German Emperor</b>, and the Treaty of Frankfurt that May annexed Alsace-Lorraine and imposed a five-billion-franc indemnity that helped fund early industrial expansion.</blockquote><a href="/empire/german-empire">German Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>After 1871 the King of Prussia was also <b>German Emperor</b>, the Prussian minister-president was usually the Imperial Chancellor, and Prussia held seventeen of the fifty-eight votes in the Federal Council (Bundesrat), enough to block constitutional change.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-prussia">Kingdom of Prussia · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Outside the empire the melody served Prussia (1795–1918), the <b>German Emperor</b> (1871–1918), the Russian Empire (1816–1833) and Switzerland (until 1961), and it is still used for Liechtenstein's national anthem, Norway's royal anthem and the American song &quot;My Country, 'Tis of Thee&quot;.</blockquote><a href="/songs/god-save-the-king">God Save the King</a></li><li><blockquote>The Dutch government rejected Allied demands to extradite the former <b>German Emperor</b> for trial under the Treaty of Versailles.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/january-23">23 January in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Victoria, the Princess Royal, married the future <b>German Emperor</b> Frederick III at St James's Palace.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/january-25">25 January in history</a></li><li><blockquote>The confederation was renamed the German Empire, and William I was proclaimed <b>German Emperor</b> at Versailles on 18 January 1871.</blockquote><a href="/state/north-german-confederation">North German Confederation</a></li></ul></section>
